WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.590 --> 00:00:04.330
Having a movie made about your life might
seem like a modern dream, but for those who've

00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:08.040
watched their stories play on the big screen,
it can sometimes be a nightmare.

00:00:08.429 --> 00:00:12.280
For these real life people who were depicted
in movies, the experience of becoming film

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.560
famous was not all it was cracked up to be.

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:15.680
Here's why.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:16.880
Moneyball

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:21.650
If you're unfamiliar with the story of Moneyball,
it's a retelling of how Oakland Athletics

00:00:21.650 --> 00:00:26.160
general manager Billy Beane reshaped the way
professional baseball scouts and front offices

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.830
put together a competitive roster.

00:00:28.220 --> 00:00:32.820
The movie was critically acclaimed, but Oakland's
real-life manager Art Howe was not pleased

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:33.540
with what he saw.

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.730
Howe was portrayed in the movie by Philip
Seymour Hoffman, and while Hoffman may have

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:41.440
been an Oscar-winning prestige actor, Howe
thought he was too hefty to play him.

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.440
But that was the smallest of his concerns
with the depiction.

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:48.260
Howe also took issue with the fact that he
was represented as little more than an old

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:52.280
school baseball dinosaur who was being lapped
by brilliant minds like Beane.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:55.600
He even called the movie "character assassination,"
saying,

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.600
"People who don’t know me are going to think
that’s how I was.

00:00:58.900 --> 00:01:02.620
You work to build a reputation and then in
two hours this movie breaks it down."

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:06.909
He was also less than impressed with the movie's
attention to players' on-base percentages

00:01:06.909 --> 00:01:09.680
because, in his words, that was a "duh" moment.

00:01:10.720 --> 00:01:12.160
“No, seriously, that is.”

00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:17.180
The Blind Side

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.399
For Baltimore Ravens' offensive lineman Michael
Oher, all the buzz that followed his portrayal

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:24.740
in The Blind Side was a bit infuriating.

00:01:25.369 --> 00:01:29.060
While it was true that the Tuohys took him
in and have since become his family, the movie

00:01:29.071 --> 00:01:33.079
made it seem as though he was a gentle giant
with protective instincts whom Sandra Bullock

00:01:33.080 --> 00:01:34.900
guided into getting good at football.

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:39.320
But in real life he'd been playing football
for years before he was brought into the Tuohy

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:39.820
crew.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:41.120
As he told NPR,

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.860
"...I could not figure out why the director
chose to show me as someone who had to be

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:46.040
taught the game of football.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:50.319
Whether it was S.J. moving around ketchup
bottles or Leigh Anne explaining to me what

00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.400
blocking is about, I watched those scenes
thinking, 'No, that's not me at all!

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:58.100
I've been studying, really studying, the game
since I was a kid!'

00:01:58.500 --> 00:02:00.140
That was my main hang-up with the film."

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:02.100
"Well alright then."

00:02:02.820 --> 00:02:03.820
All Eyez on Me

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:09.039
This bio-pic of Tupac Shakur was a critical
miss for several reasons, not the least of

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:12.980
which was how the movie recreated Jada Pinkett
Smith's involvement in his life story.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.599
The actress skewered the film on Twitter and
accused the filmmakers of fabricating a scene

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:21.720
wherein Shakur read her a poem and claimed
they completely invented a farewell between

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:22.220
the two.

00:02:22.540 --> 00:02:25.500
She also denied the occurrence of an argument
portrayed in the film, according to Rolling

00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.860
Stone, calling the movie's version of events
"deeply hurtful."

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:29.280
Sully

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:34.680
It's a scientific fact that everybody loves
Tom Hanks, and the man he portrays in 2016's

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.080
Sully is a bona fide hero.

00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:40.540
Captain Chesley Sullenberger III was the pilot
who pulled off the Miracle on the Hudson,

00:02:40.549 --> 00:02:45.349
so named because it was one of the most insane
emergency landings ever attempted and saved

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:46.879
every person on board the plane.

00:02:47.080 --> 00:02:50.860
But if the film's plot had been true to the
actual events, it would have been over in

00:02:50.860 --> 00:02:55.890
half an hour because all that time spent investigating
whether or not he'd done right thing was greatly

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:56.580
exaggerated.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:00.280
There was never any significant doubt as to
whether Sully made the right call.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:04.560
The first set of simulations, shown in the
film to constitute damning evidence that the

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.700
emergency landing was unnecessary, showed
just the opposite.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.240
And the final report praised both Sully and
the crew for their heroism.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.780
The real investigators were none too happy
about this, accusing director Clint Eastwood

00:03:15.780 --> 00:03:19.540
of making manufactured villains out of them
for the sake of dramatic tension.

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:23.730
Now-retired lead investigator Robert Benzon
complained that the film had hurt his reputation,

00:03:23.730 --> 00:03:28.120
while putting its level of realism "somewhere
between Sharknado 2 and Sharknado 3."

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:29.560
Spotlight

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:34.880
In an age where news outlets consistently
get accused of alleged bias and fakery, Spotlight

00:03:34.880 --> 00:03:37.820
provides a noble portrait of journalistic
courage and integrity.

00:03:38.020 --> 00:03:42.370
The Oscar-winning movie revisited how the
Boston Globe exposed heinous sexual abuse

00:03:42.370 --> 00:03:44.680
and a dastardly cover-up by the Catholic Church.

00:03:44.780 --> 00:03:49.819
So, a picture this serious might seem to demand
a standard of truth-seeking worthy of the

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:50.920
journalists it represents.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:55.420
However, one of real-life people depicted
in the movie was more than mildly offended

00:03:55.420 --> 00:03:56.860
as to how he came off in the pic.

00:03:57.100 --> 00:04:01.540
School representative Jack Dunn was painted
as a morally ambiguous figure who tried to

00:04:01.540 --> 00:04:02.930
excuse away abuses.

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.400
"It's a big school, Robbie, you know that.

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:10.480
And then we're talking about seven, eight
victims over eight years?"

00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:15.380
The real Dunn subsequently sued the studio
for defamation, and ultimately, the filmmakers

00:04:15.389 --> 00:04:18.209
apologized to him and settled for an undisclosed
sum.

